\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Constants and odd Gaussian observables}
\date{2026-10-25}

\p{For the [Gaussian expectation](fdip-0006) associated to positive
self-adjoint #{Q} on #{(E,h)}, constants satisfy #{\omega_Q(c)=c}.
Every absolutely integrable odd Borel function #{F}, meaning
#{F(-x)=-F(x)}, has #{\omega_Q(F)=0}. In particular each linear
observable #{\ell_j(x)=h(j,x)} has mean zero.}
\proof{
\p{Normalization gives the assertion for constants. Negation is an
isometry and preserves [canonical volume](fdip-002M), by the
[orthogonal invariance](fdip-002L) argument in coordinates. Moreover
#{h(-x,Q(-x))=h(x,Qx)}, so it preserves the weight. Absolute
integrability makes substitution legitimate and gives
##{\omega_Q(F)=\omega_Q(F\circ(-I))=-\omega_Q(F).}
Thus the expectation is zero. Linear observables are integrable by
\ref{fdip-002W}. When #{E=0}, an odd function must vanish at its unique point.}
}
\p{This is a direct consequence of normalization and the change-of-variables
convention used in \citet{Exercise 1.2.21, p. 41, preliminary version}{tao2011measure}.
Oddness alone, without absolute integrability, does not define an expectation.}
